  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/b46f87399fc02d2d63199bee5f7a584c067adaf7526c085614c4e124118aae06.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/0f2c7b0dc5aafa14501aec735c74b21cc653a0b9799ff55e204fe8f8d530e1ce.png)\n\nThe point $(5,3)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(1,3)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-1=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
